AB  - Identifying surface alterations from multi-temporal remote sensing imagery is essential for tasks including disaster surveillance, land use monitoring, and evaluating urban expansion. Although recent deep learning–based methods have significantly improved detection accuracy, their heavy computational cost and large model size limit practical deployment, especially on satellites and UAVs with constrained resources. With the aim of resolving these obstacles, we design GSMFA-Net, an effective model with lightweight architecture. The model adopts MobileNetV2 as its backbone and incorporates three key modules: a Dense Feature Enhancement Module (DFEM) for cross-scale feature reinforcement, a Global Semantic Aggregation Module (GSAM) for robust contextual modeling, and a Guided Fusion Module (GFM) for self-attention–driven multi-scale fusion with semantic consistency. Evaluations on three public benchmarks show that GSMFA-Net achieves competitive detection performance while maintaining only 3.28 M parameters and 4.78G FLOPs, striking a favorable balance between efficiency and accuracy. Qualitative comparisons further confirm its capability to detect small-scale and structurally complex changes while effectively suppressing false positives. Overall, this study highlights the potential of GSMFA-Net as a practical solution for accurate and efficient remote sensing change detection under strict resource constraints.
ER  -

