AB  - Monitoring water stored in reservoirs is essential for sustainable water resource management. In Mexico, federal agencies generate information on the quantity and quality of surface and groundwater; however, datasets are often incomplete due to monitoring gaps or data failures. The Bacurato and El Sabinal dams supply water for municipal use and one of the largest irrigation districts in the country, yet historical records of water volumes are available only from 1991 and 1995, despite their operations starting in 1981 and 1985, respectively. Calculating dam water volumes typically requires curves relating flooded surface areas to storage volumes; however, these curves were unavailable, limiting traditional methods. To address this limitation, we developed and evaluated a methodology that integrates flooded surface areas (derived from spectral indices computed in Google Earth Engine) with in-situ volume measurements using artificial intelligence models (Random Forest, Extreme Gradient Boosting, and a Deep Neural Network for Regression) and polynomial regression. Among these, the deep neural network achieved the best performance. The AWEInsh water index provided the most accurate flooded area delineation, with an overall accuracy of 99.5 %. The deep neural network attained coefficients of determination above 0.99 during validation and above 0.95 with independent data. These results demonstrate the potential of combining Landsat imagery, spectral indices and artificial intelligence to accurately estimate dam water volumes in northern Sinaloa, Mexico, thereby supporting informed water management and policy decisions.
