AB  - Significance As a new type of pollutant, the environmental pollution caused by microplastics has attracted extensive attention from the international community. The UN Environment Programme is calling for increased scientific research on microplastics and the development of pollution prevention and control measures. Scientific detection technology is the basis for assessing the current status of microplastic pollution and developing effective control measures. In recent years, the research on microplastic detection technologies and methods has made some progress, but there are still great challenges in realizing rapid and accurate detection. At present, common methods for detecting the components of microplastics both at home and abroad include visual analysis, Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR), Raman spectroscopy, hyperspectral imaging (HSI) analysis, and thermal analysis, etc. Each of them has its own advantages and disadvantages in the identification of microplastic components, and there are significant gaps in the rapid and efficient identification and analysis of full-size microplastics in the environment. The accuracy of microplastic component detection depends on strict preconditions and standardized pretreatment processes. The collection, classification, and storage of samples are important steps before testing, and organic matter, minerals, and non-plastic particles in complex environmental samples need to be pre-treated to remove interference. The quality of pretreatment directly affects the accuracy of subsequent detection methods, especially in marine environments where complex samples pose higher requirements for pretreatment. In-situ detection of marine microplastics is of great significance for the study of their actual spatial distribution, migration paths, aggregation mechanisms in different water layers, and food chain enrichment. However, the development and research of on-site detection equipment for microplastics still face significant technical challenges at present, including the portability and adaptability of the equipment, and its effective analytical capability for complex marine environments. Recently, with the continuous advancement of detection technology, emerging detection technologies represented by polarization methods have demonstrated significant advantages in the field of microplastic detection. Polarization measurement can quickly identify microplastics without damaging the sample, and it also has good anti-interference ability against environmental background noise, making it particularly suitable for non-destructive testing in complex marine environments. Compared with traditional methods, polarization technology has demonstrated obvious potential and advantages in the rapid, on-site, and precise detection of marine microplastics, which provides an important tool for studying the spatial distribution, dynamic migration paths, and interactions with ecosystems of microplastics. Progress The detection technologies and methods research on microplastics are summarized. First, visual analysis can conduct rapid preliminary screening and detection of microplastics. It is a traditional method for identifying and quantifying microplastics and is widely used due to its simple operation, low cost, and relatively small chemical hazard. However, visual analysis cannot determine the chemical composition of microplastics and is easily influenced by the subjective judgment of the operator, leading to misjudgment and missed detection. FTIR is one of the most widely used methods for analyzing the chemical composition of microplastics, which can meet the demand for rapid scanning. It has the advantages of extremely short scanning time (about 1 s), strong signal, high sensitivity, and compact instrumentation. Raman spectroscopy features high spatial resolution, strong specificity, and non-destructive detection. When a laser of a specific wavelength is shone on an object, the intensity of the bands in the emitted light that differ from the incident wavelength can be measured to obtain a "wavelength-intensity" Raman spectrum, which can then be used to analyze the composition of the substance. HSI technology can capture the surface spectral features of objects, providing two-dimensional spatial information for spectral profiles in specific scenes. It can obtain detailed spectral information contained in each pixel and provide a more comprehensive analysis of the physical and chemical properties of the sample. Thermal analysis can provide effective information such as the type, component percentage, and mass of polymers. However, it is destructive and can only characterize the chemical composition, but cannot obtain information such as the morphology, size, and quantity distribution of microplastics. The pyrolysis products are generally volatile and exhibit obvious mass loss. Polarization is one of the fundamental properties of light. Currently, polarization measurement has emerged as a new microplastic detection technology due to its ability to carry information on the morphology, structure, and other characteristics of the particles being detected, and is widely applied in atmospheric and oceanic measurement fields. Polarization measurement methods include measuring the Stokes vector of the scattered light of an object or directly measuring the Mueller matrix of the object. This method can be divided into two approaches for measuring marine particulate matter: one-dimensional single-particle polarization measurement and two-dimensional polarization imaging. This paper reviews the application cases of polarization measurement in the detection of marine microplastic samples in recent years, demonstrating that it has very good application prospects. Conclusions and Prospects The demand for microplastic detection has grown significantly worldwide. Existing detection technologies struggle to keep pace with increasing detection demands, but the polarization method is undoubtedly one of the more notable technologies. The development of future microplastic detection technology requires the integration of rapid, automatic detection and analysis with artificial intelligence technology.
