AB  - A novel method based on combining spectral in-dices with machine learning and deep learning approaches was explored to obtain accurate deforestation mapping from multi-temporal satellite images. First, healthy vegetation is extracted with structural features using a joint processing of Sentinel-2 and Sentinel-1 sensors' optical and SAR data, respectively. Specifically, our process begins with the application of the RF classifier to identify the main land cover types, making use of spectral and radar indicators. However, such information is obtained under conditions of weak supervision based on the use of NDVI thresholds. Further enhancement of the output spatial map has been achieved through the use of a CNN, which detects dependencies in patches. Although we rely on the change detection maps based on NDVI, the forest cover loss is confirmed through NDVI time series analysis. In this way, due to combining the capabilities of the RF and CNN algorithms, our multi-layer classification system enables increasing accuracy and spatial consistency without using any training data. To assist with environmental review and legislative planning, the platform also features a comprehensive dashboard for the visualization of the results. Thus, this work showcases how multimodal data integration and learning under low supervision can help rethink the modern ecological monitoring.
