AB  - Rapid and accurate flood mapping using Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) imagery is a critical tool for emergency response. While machine learning (ML) models have significantly enhanced the accuracy and efficiency of flood mapping, their “black box” nature limits their trustworthiness for critical emergency response applications. To address this issue, we developed an interpretable flood mapping framework based on Gaofen-3 (GF-3) SAR imagery that fuses remote sensing physics with data-driven analysis. The framework comprises three core components: (1) a novel physics-guided feature, the Generalized Normalized Difference Polarization Index (GNDPI), which transforms skewed backscatter data into a more separable bimodal distribution; (2) a Random Forest (RF) classification model incorporating multi-source features, including Gray-Level Co-occurrence Matrix (GLCM), Local Binary Pattern (LBP), and Haar Wavelet; and (3) the SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) method, utilized to interpret the model’s decisions. The framework was successfully applied to the Dongdian flood retention area (DFRA) during China’s “23·7″ major flood event, yielding high accuracy (Overall Accuracy (OA) = 97.72%, Kappa = 0.953). In model comparisons, the RF model demonstrated over four times the computational efficiency of XGBoost, with a minimal 0.11% loss in accuracy, proving its practical value for emergency scenarios. Crucially, SHAP analysis revealed that the model learned a physically consistent decision logic, prioritizing features for macroscopic smoothness and microscopic uniformity, and leveraging complex feature interactions to differentiate water from non-water pixels. This study’s framework not only improves flood mapping performance but also demystifies the model’s decision logic via SHAP, offering a transparent and robust tool for rapid emergency response in data-scarce basins.
