AB  - Accurate estimation of above-ground biomass (AGB) is fundamental for understanding carbon dynamics, assessing forest health, and guiding conservation strategies. However, mapping AGB in structurally complex old-growth forests remains challenging due to limitations in remote sensing data and modeling approaches. This study presents the first evaluation of the newly released AlphaEarth Foundation (AEF) embeddings (a global, analysis-ready dataset derived from multimodal Earth observation sources) for AGB estimation. Using 2543 field plots from the Hyrcanian forests of northern Iran, six machine learning algorithms (MLs), Random Forest (RF), Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), Support Vector Regression (SVR), k-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Linear Regression (LR), and Artificial Neural Networks (ANN) were evaluated, together with a weighted ensemble approach. The correction of plot locations using a canopy height model derived from Meta AI models significantly reduced geolocation bias, underscoring the importance of spatial alignment between field and satellite data. Feature importance analysis showed that a subset of layers strongly contributed to predictive power. Across algorithms, non-linear models slightly outperformed linear methods, with XGBoost and ANN achieving the highest accuracies (R2 ≈ 0.63 after geolocation correction). Ensemble modeling further improved prediction robustness (R2 ≈ 0.71), reducing error metrics and producing spatially consistent AGB maps at 10 m resolution. These findings highlight the value of AEF embeddings as a novel feature set for AGB mapping in complex forest ecosystems. This work demonstrates how foundation models, combined with ensemble learning, can support high-resolution, scalable AGB monitoring and provide essential tools for forest management, carbon accounting, and biodiversity conservation.
