AB  - Mangrove ecosystems are critical for coastal protection, yet their rapid loss poses significant challenges. We develop a novel hybrid forecasting framework integrating Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) with CNN-LSTM architectures to predict mangrove presence in East Luwu, South Sulawesi, Indonesia. Our key innovation lies in embedding governing partial differential equations-incorporating advection, diffusion, and logistic growth-directly into the neural network loss function, ensuring predictions satisfy fundamental conservation laws while leveraging deep learning’s pattern recognition capabilities. Using Landsat and Global Mangrove Watch data (1996–2020), we construct spatiotemporal sequences processed through a CNN-LSTM encoder that extracts latent features, which are then fused with geographic coordinates in a PINN decoder. Training over 4000 epochs with Adam (learning rate 0.001) followed by LBFGS optimization achieved loss reduction from 1.10 to 0.25, with diffusion coefficient D and growth rate r converging to (Formula presented) m2/s and (Formula presented) year−1[jls-end-space/], respectively, indicating ecosystem equilibrium. Comparative evaluation demonstrates our approach outperforms baseline models: validation metrics show IoU  ≈  0.015 and Dice  ≈  0.030 (17 % improvement over pure CNN-LSTM), with MSE  ≈  0.65 and RMSE  ≈  0.81. Physical validation confirms model adherence to conservation principles, with continuity residuals (Formula presented) s−1 and momentum residuals (Formula presented) N/m3, while predicted drag coefficients (0.85-1.38) align with empirical ranges (0.8-1.4). Ablation studies reveal the physics-informed component contributes 25 % reduction in prediction error compared to data-driven approaches alone. This work advances physics-informed machine learning for ecological forecasting and provides an interpretable, scientifically consistent tool for coastal ecosystem management.
