AB  - Although Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) are widely used in hyperspectral image classification, standard architectures often fail to fully exploit the multiscale characteristics of spectral-spatial data. To bridge this gap, we propose a Wavelet-based Multiscale Feature Enhancement Network (W2MFEN), which synergizes the multi-resolution analysis of wavelet transform with the representation learning of CNNs. Unlike meaningful single-stream networks, W2MFEN decomposes data into varying frequency components, enabling the simultaneous extraction of global semantic contours and local high-frequency details. To ensure feature purity, we design a Global Noise Suppression Attention module that specifically targets and attenuates noise in high-frequency spatial bands. Additionally, a Local Feature Dimensionality Reduction Attention module is introduced to optimize the spectral dimension, reducing redundancy while enhancing local feature saliency. These diverse features are ultimately synthesized by a Multiscale Feature Fusion Enhancement module, which adaptively weights and aggregates information for precise classification. Extensive experiments verify that our method effectively handles multiscale variations and outperforms existing mainstream models.
