AB  - Accurate estimation of daily air temperature is vital for climate monitoring, agricultural planning, and environmental management, particularly in regions with limited meteorological data. This study presents a modeling framework that integrates satellite-derived land surface temperature (LST) from MODIS sensors with environmental variables from the ERA5 reanalysis to estimate daily maximum, minimum, and mean air temperatures across Zanjan Province, Iran, for the period 2018–2022. A Random Forest (RF) machine learning model was developed and compared with a Multiple Linear Regression (MLR) model using ground-based station observations for validation. After model optimization and data quality control, the RF achieved root-mean-square errors (RMSE) of 2.99 °C, 1.88 °C, and 1.85 °C for maximum, minimum, and mean air temperatures, respectively, outperforming the MLR model in all cases. Feature importance analysis identified MODIS LST as the most influential predictor, followed by soil moisture. Seasonal evaluation showed the highest model accuracy in autumn and spring, while reduced performance was mainly observed for maximum air temperature during summer. As an independent case study, the RF model trained over the 2018–2022 period was applied to a representative summer day (10 July 2023), producing realistic spatial temperature patterns at 1-km resolution. Validation against station observations demonstrated strong agreement for minimum and mean temperatures (R > 0.90) and moderate agreement for maximum temperature (R = 0.65). These findings highlight the effectiveness of integrating satellite observations with machine learning techniques for high-resolution air temperature estimation in regions with limited observational coverage.
