AB  - PT Freeport Indonesia operates one of the world's largest copper and gold mines in the highlands of Papua, where open-pit extraction converts vegetated land to barren ground and requires systematic reclamation. This study analysed spatiotemporal changes in vegetation cover within the Grasberg Reclamation Area using Sentinel-2 imagery from 2016 to 2025. Vegetation greenness was quantified through the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI), and vegetation trend composition was examined across topographic classes, including elevation, slope, and aspect. Mean NDVI increased from 0.081 in 2016 to 0.357 in 2025, and moderate-to-high greenness expanded from 1.88% to 56.79% of the valid reclaimed area. Sen's Slope and Mann-Kendall trend analysis showed that a significant increase was the dominant trend class, covering 82.59% of the analysed reclaimed area, and total increasing trends covered 93.97%. Declining trends accounted for only 0.12% of the area. The dominant recovery class was weak recovery (23.49%), while strong and very strong recovery together represented 46.98% of the reclaimed area. Across slope classes, total increasing trends ranged from 89.75% on flat terrain to 96.78% on very steep terrain. Among aspect classes, southeast-facing slopes had the highest significant increase (89.11%), while flat and northwest classes showed the lowest proportions. Elevation classes showed broadly similar trend compositions, with total increase ranging from 92.80% to 94.82% across quartile classes. These findings provide a scientific basis for improving revegetation management in alpine mine reclamation areas, particularly in tropical mountainous environments where topographic effects on vegetation recovery remain poorly documented.
