AB  - Conservation tillage practices play critical roles in sustainable agriculture and have become more popular in the United States (US). Accurate spatial and temporal maps about tillage practices are essential to monitoring conservation tillage adoption and assessing its impacts on soil water and nutrient loss and greenhouse gas emissions. We built a dynamic feature threshold framework utilizing satellite data, environmental variables, and machine learning to map out tillage practices for corn and soybean fields in the Midwest from 2000 to 2022. The framework can effectively consider the confounding impact from local soil, vegetation, and environmental effects on crop residues. We compared tillage mapping accuracy using dynamic feature threshold or fixed feature threshold with either broadband angle index (BAI) or normalized difference tillage index (NDTI) as the key feature for tillage. The results showed that the dynamic feature threshold framework paired with BAI feature can accurately estimate tillage percentage for three categorical practices (no-till, reduced-till, and conventional-till), with R2 of 0.84, 0.62, and 0.76 for no-till, reduced-till, and conventional-till in corn fields, and corresponding R2 of 0.84, 0.52, and 0.70 in soybean fields, respectively. The dynamic feature threshold framework achieved better performance with the BAI-based feature than the NDTI-based feature, with R2 increasing by 0.03–0.12 for different corn tillage practices and by 0.05–0.10 for soybean. The temporally fixed feature threshold framework has a much lower performance than the dynamic feature threshold framework (R2 reduced by 0.23–0.61 for the accuracy to map different categories of tillage for corn and soybean), mainly because it does not consider the confounding factors. Based on the results from the dynamics feature threshold framework with BAI, we found significant regional differences of tillage adoption in the US Midwest. We also found that the adoption of conservation tillage (no-till or reduced-till) increases slowly from 2000 to 2022 across the U.S. Midwest. Northern counties in Dakotas, southern Minnesota, eastern Nebraska, and Iowa showed an increase trend in no-till percentage, which largely were converted from reduced-till percentage. Some counties in Indiana and Ohio showed an increase trend in reduced-till percentage, mainly converted from conventional- and no-till practices. The proposed framework based on remote sensing leads to much improved tillage information in space and time as demonstrated in the US Midwest, and provides a highly applicable and cost-effective way for regional tillage practice mapping.
