AB  - Visible (RGB) and thermal infrared (TIR) feature complementarity is vital for maritime monitoring, particularly in coastal environments with complex lighting and weather conditions. However, mainstream existing multispectral feature fusion methods rely on static topological strategies, which cannot adapt to rapid changes in modality reliability, causing cross-modal feature contamination and redundant computation over large backgrounds. To address these issues, this paper proposes a highly efficient and dynamically intelligent multispectral detection framework called CMRNet. Specifically, to tackle cross-modal feature contamination during fusion, we design modality complementary differential fusion, a new fusion paradigm inspired by common-mode rejection in differential amplifiers. By explicitly modeling modal differential potential energy, it builds dynamic reliability allocation and suppresses heterogeneous noise propagation. Considering the spatial redundancy of unstructured sea surfaces, we design a polymorphic convblock that routes computation according to scene complexity, allocating network capacity to complex regions. Furthermore, to prevent tiny objects from being overwhelmed by vast backgrounds, we design a spatial density prior to guide query selection toward potential targets. Experiments on SeaRGBT-Tiny, MSRS, FLIR, and LLVIP show that our method achieves excellent performance. On SeaRGBT-Tiny, it obtains 51.9% AP (+6.0%), requiring only 3.60M parameters and 3.01 ms latency. Code and datasets are available at https://github.com/Toclimbbb/CMRNet.
