AB  - Aquaculture is integral to both food security and the economic framework of Vietnam; however, the sustainability of this sector is increasingly jeopardized by environmental degradation and climate change, necessitating the implementation of sophisticated monitoring and risk assessment methodologies. This research utilizes Sentinel-2 satellite imagery, Artificial Neural Network (ANN) models, and a hybrid Genetic Algorithm-Extreme Gradient Boosting (GA-XGBoost) model to delineate aquaculture zones and evaluate environmental susceptibility across four significant socio-economic regions. The classification outcomes indicated that the nationwide aquaculture area totals 1,204,600 ha, with the Mekong River Delta (MRD) accounting for a substantial 77.44%, predominantly attributed to intensive shrimp aquaculture practices. Nonetheless, the MRD has also been identified as highly vulnerable in its aquaculture zones, primarily due to the compounded effects of saline intrusion during dry periods coupled with abrupt environmental shifts in the rainy season. In contrast, the North Central and Central Coastal (NCCC) region displayed a consistent vulnerability pattern along waterways, influenced by flooding and storm activities. Notably, vulnerability hotspots in the Red River Delta (RRD) and South East (SE) regions were concentrated around major coastal urban and industrial hubs, largely driven by wastewater contamination and urban heat island phenomena. The GA-XGBoost model demonstrated remarkable accuracy (R2train > 0.83) in forecasting vulnerability in aquaculture areas by incorporating both natural and anthropogenic variables and pinpointed optimal aquaculture sites, including the freshwater-brackish transition zones in Tien Giang and Tra Vinh, as well as the stable lagoon areas in Thua Thien Hue, which maintain consistent environmental conditions throughout the year. These insights offer a vital scientific basis for the strategic planning of sustainable aquaculture zones, fostering aquaculture practices that are both effective and resilient in the face of escalating environmental challenges.
