AB  - Accurate object detection in remote sensing images (RSIs) is crucial for a wide range of engineering applications, from environmental monitoring to disaster response, where high precision is necessary for reliable decision-making. Recent research in RSI object detection has increasingly leveraged Vision Transformer (ViT)-based backbones and feature fusion techniques to enhance global context extraction. However, these methods often struggle with the inherent challenges of RSIs, such as variable imaging conditions and high morphological diversity, leading to inconsistent performance compared to pure convolutional models while incurring high computational overhead. To overcome these limitations, we propose a Feature Enhancement and Structural Simplification Network (FESS-Net) that integrates hybrid data-, model-, and loss-level innovations into the Faster R-CNN architecture. At the data level, FESS-Net introduces two RSI-specific feature enhancement pipelines integrated with custom algorithms: Remote Sensing Optical Enhancement to simulate diverse imaging conditions and Remote Sensing Shape Enhancement to enrich object shape variability. At the model level, it introduces a Lightweight Hybrid ViT-based Backbone and a Lightweight Context-Aware Fusion Module for streamlined feature extraction and fusion, as well as a Center-Aware Loss to enhance spatial alignment during training. Extensive evaluations on three benchmarks—DIOR20, MAR20, and ShipRS50—yield outstanding mAP50 scores of 80.5%, 96.2%, and 76.1%, respectively, significantly outperforming foundational baselines (Faster R-CNN, YOLOv11, and RT-DETR) and 17 advanced variant detectors published in top-tier journals between 2024 and 2026. Furthermore, evaluations on the practical, real-world GLH-Bridge dataset demonstrate superior cross-scene generalizability compared to YOLOv11 and RT-DETR baselines. Although FESS-Net exhibits a higher latency of 20.4 ms (49 FPS) compared to YOLOv11-m, it still comfortably exceeds the standard real-time processing threshold of 30 FPS. Additionally, cross-architecture evaluations using YOLOv11 and RT-DETR substantiate the robust plug-and-play generality of our proposed feature enhancement modules. These results confirm that FESS-Net establishes an optimal trade-off between detection accuracy and efficiency for practical remote sensing applications.
