AB  - Developing a practical and robust cloud removal method has long been a crucial research objective in the field of remote sensing. Existing multi-temporal approaches typically adopt cloud-free images from other acquisition dates as training targets. However, the inherent temporal heterogeneity of optical observations introduces intrinsic limitations to this paradigm. To overcome these issues, we propose a novel cloud removal strategy based on a Generate-to-Remove (G2R) self-supervised framework. Unlike conventional methods, G2R employs the cloudy image itself as the supervision target, thereby introducing temporal constraints into the reconstruction process and enabling faithful recovery of the original surface features beneath cloud-covered regions. Furthermore, to address the insufficient capability of existing multi-temporal networks in modeling spatio-spectral dynamics and cross-dimensional interactions, we design the Spectral–Spatial Mamba network (SSMamba). Built upon a structured state space model, SSMamba adopts dual spectral and spatial branches tailored for multi-temporal inputs, achieving coordinated representation learning and efficient feature interaction. This design allows for fully exploiting spectral consistency and spatial dependencies across temporal observations, leading to improved detail restoration and stronger generalization. Experimental results on multiple real-image scenes demonstrate that the proposed framework not only achieves accurate reconstruction of cloud-covered regions but also delivers substantial advantages over state-of-the-art approaches, highlighting its strong potential for operational applications.
