AB  - AbstractHigh-resolution images are crucial for remote sensing applications, as they directly influence the accuracy of target detection and scene interpretation. Compared with natural image super-resolution, remote sensing image super-resolution requires greater attention to high-frequency details, such as edges, as well as the reconstruction of multi-scale objects within complex backgrounds. In this paper, we propose a super-resolution network named the edge enhancement and high-frequency aware network (EHFNet) for remote sensing images. An edge enhancement module and a multi-scale attention module are designed to extract powerful features, enabling the network to capture sharp structural information while effectively modeling multi-scale contextual dependencies. In addition, a novel loss function that combines edge-domain and frequency-domain components is introduced to better reconstruct fine details and high-frequency information. Experiments on three benchmark datasets demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms existing approaches in both reconstruction quality and downstream tasks, such as object detection and scene classification.
