AB  - Estimating the gravity direction from a single aerial image provides an absolute vertical prior, which is valuable for spatial reasoning when multi-view stereo or external spatial references are unavailable. Although onboard inertial measurement units (IMUs) provide platform attitude, transferring these measurements to the camera coordinate frame requires precise synchronization and accurate extrinsic calibration, which are not always satisfied for low-cost aerial platforms. Moreover, such calibration information is often unavailable for crowdsourced or historical aerial imagery. These limitations make vision-based approaches a practical alternative for estimating gravity direction directly from images. However, reliable estimation from a single aerial image remains challenging due to the wide range of aerial viewing angles, which often leads to ambiguous geometric cues. In this work, we propose Aerial Image Gravity Direction Estimation (AGE), a framework for estimating gravity direction from a single aerial image. To resolve geometric ambiguity under diverse viewing configurations, AGE combines semantically rich features from a frozen DINOv2 backbone with camera-aware geometric features derived from known camera intrinsics. Geometric reasoning is guided by supervision from an intermediate Perspective Field, while gravity is predicted as a 3D unit vector for stable learning across aerial viewpoints. To facilitate training and evaluation, we construct AGE-Pano, a large-scale synthetic dataset containing 28,500 images with accurate ground-truth gravity directions and diverse camera viewpoints and intrinsics. Experiments on both synthetic and real-world aerial imagery demonstrate state-of-the-art accuracy and robustness under aerial viewing conditions.
