AB  - Vegetation indices (VIs) are fundamental tools for assessing vegetation health, productivity, and ecosystem changes under growing climate and land-use pressures. Reliable forecasting of these indices is essential for sustainable resource management and climate adaptation. However, accurate VI prediction remains challenging due to the nonlinear, nonstationary, and spatiotemporal nature of vegetation dynamics. Most existing deep learning models predict single-point or regional-average values, neglecting spatial continuity and inter-pixel dependencies needed for accurate map-based forecasting. Conversely, methods that generate spatially explicit VI maps often perform inconsistently across heterogeneous landscapes that combine forests, water bodies, urban zones, and bare soil. These challenges highlight the need for architectures that integrate local and global spatial context while preserving temporal coherence across diverse environments. This paper introduces a hybrid ConvLSTM-GCN-Transformer architecture for high-resolution VI forecasting from time-series data. ConvLSTM captures temporal evolution, the Graph Convolutional Network (GCN) models local spatial dependencies, and the Transformer enhances global feature representation. Trained on monthly NDVI maps from Landsat imagery (1996-2025) over northern Tunisia, the model achieved an RMSE of 0.034 and an NSE of 0.866, outperforming baseline models. Two generalization studies were conducted to evaluate robustness: (i) cross-sensor evaluation using Sentinel-2 and MODIS imagery (RMSEs of 0.064 and 0.09), and (ii) cross-index adaptation to the Enhanced Vegetation Index (EVI) and Soil-Adjusted Vegetation Index (SAVI). All codes are publicly available to support reproducibility (Link here).
