AB  - Salient Object Detection (SOD) in Hyperspectral images (HSI) aims to identify the most spectrally distinctive regions in an image and is widely used in applications such as remote sensing, environmental monitoring, and precision agriculture. Existing methods often rely on location priors, making them sensitive to object position in the image, or depend on similarity metrics, making them computationally complex. Deep learning (DL) based approaches have also been explored, but they are generally data-intensive, and the limited availability of large-scale HSOD datasets constrain their applicability. To address these limitations, we propose a location-independent and similarity-metric-agnostic framework that jointly exploits spectral and spatial information. The Isolation Forest (IF), a machine learning algorithm, is used to identify pixels with anomalous spectral signatures, followed by weighted connected component analysis on the intermediate binary saliency map to obtain a coherent salient object. The Minimum Noise Fraction (MNF) transform is further applied to enhance anomalous spectral behavior while reducing noise. Experiments on the HS-SOD dataset show that the proposed method achieves a precision of 0.69, recall of 0.49, and F1-score of 0.54 at a 12% pixel threshold, outperforming state-of-the-art approaches. The method is also computationally efficient, with sensitivity analysis indicating nearly a threefold improvement in runtime, requiring only 0.38 s compared to 1.40 s for existing methods.
