AB  - This study presents an integrated spatio-temporal assessment of ecological restoration in the Jharia Coalfield using multi-temporal dataset acquired between 2005 and 2024. A comprehensive remote sensing framework integrating spectral indices, including NDVI, NDMI, NDWI, Bare Soil Index (BSI), Land Surface Temperature (LST), and Land Use and Land Cover (LULC) analysis, was developed to evaluate long-term ecological transformations in one of India's most intensively mined coalfields. Temporal trends were quantified using the Mann–Kendall test and Sen's slope estimator, while an Analytical Hierarchy Process (AHP)-based Ecological Restoration Index (ERI) was formulated with high decision consistency (CR = 0.04). The findings reveal substantial mining-induced landscape modification over the past two decades. Mining areas increased from 24.90 km2 to 54.86 km2, while built-up land expanded from 31.85 km2 to 44.82 km2, reflecting rapid industrial and urban growth. In contrast, vegetation cover declined from 100.91 km2 to 94.50 km2, although localized recovery was observed in reclaimed sectors. Barren land decreased from 145.77 km2 in 2015 to 112.71 km2 in 2024, indicating measurable reclamation progress. Spatial analysis further highlighted heterogeneity in restoration patterns, with severe vegetation degradation concentrated in the western mining zone (−12.4%) and ecological recovery identified in eastern agricultural regions (+18.7%). ERI classification indicates that 50.94% of the study area falls within high to very high restoration categories, whereas 18.39% remains under low restoration conditions. A statistically significant positive relationship between ERI values and distance from mining sites (R2 = 0.67, p < 0.001) confirms the dominant influence of anthropogenic disturbance on ecological recovery. Persistent thermal anomalies associated with underground coal-fire zones, reaching maximum LST values of 94.35°C, continue to impede restoration effectiveness. The proposed ERI framework provides a scalable and transferable approach for monitoring ecological restoration in mining-impacted landscapes worldwide.
