AB  - Hyperspectral images(HSIs) are susceptible to mixed noise during acquisition. Most existing denoising methods the rank function using the nuclear norm. However, this approach fails to effectively suppress noise as it imposes the same penalty on singular values. This paper employs the γ-norm from non-convex functions for rank approximation, which enables a more accurate approximation of low rank. Furthermore, this paper takes into account the local characteristics of HSIs and introduces a regularization term known as the local gradient product, which better captures the edge information and fine-scale features of the image. Consequently, by combining the γ-norm with the local gradient product(LGP), this paper proposes a HSI denoising model based on global variational γ-approximation. This model not only removes mixed noise but also preserves structural information in HSIs to the greatest extent possible. Furthermore, the Alternating Direction Method of Multipliers (ADMM) optimization algorithm is employed to solve this model. To validate the effectiveness and denoising performance of the proposed model, extensive experiments were conducted on both simulated and real-world datasets, with further experiments carried out for downstream classification tasks. The experimental results demonstrate that the model performs well in removing mixed noise.
