AB  - Nearshore green tide poses severe threats to marine ecosystems, yet accurate detection from unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) hyperspectral imagery remains challenging due to spectral confusion, irregular spatial distribution, and limited training samples. This study develops a domain-specific model MOSS-GAN tailored for green tide detection under these domain-specific constraints. The proposed method integrates three collaboratively designed components: an Octave Convolution (OctConv) branch with spatial attention to enhance edge-aware representations, a Mamba-based spectral encoder with lightweight attention to model discriminative wavelength dependencies, and a dual-branch Generative adversarial network (GAN) module that acts as a feature-space co-optimizer to enforce robust representations under data scarcity. All experiments were conducted on a real UAV hyperspectral dataset acquired in Qinhuangdao coastal waters. The method achieves 97.30% overall accuracy and a 0.9460 Kappa coefficient on the test set, outperforming conventional machine learning methods and state-of-the-art deep learning approaches. Cross-region generalization tests confirm its stability under varying green tide distributions. This work provides an accurate and operationally feasible solution for nearshore green tide monitoring, and demonstrates a reusable adversarial feature-learning paradigm for other remote sensing tasks with spectral ambiguity and limited samples.
