AB  - Study region: The Tangsige Marsh Wetland is situated in the Yanjiga River Basin, Inner Mongolia, China, a semi-arid area under climatic pressure and coal mining disturbance. Study focus: We designed an integrated framework that synergistically combines multi-decadal Landsat time-series analysis (Random Forest), XGBoost-SHAP driver attribution, and stable isotope tracers (δD, δ18O, d-excess) with a Bayesian mixing model (MixSIAR) to diagnose hydrological drivers and water sources of the wetland. New hydrological insights: Wetland area declined by 35.5% from 2000 to 2022, exhibiting four distinct stages of decline and recovery. DEM and NDVI dominate wetland distribution. Key drivers—NDVI, population density, and precipitation—exhibit nonlinear thresholds, and synchronous changes in their SHAP values correspond to the different evolutionary stages. Precipitation (47.9%) and groundwater (35.3%) are the dominant water sources, with coal mine drainage contributing 7.6% on average but reaching nearly 10% locally. A south-to-north gradient from groundwater-dominated to strongly evaporated precipitation-dominated explains the northern wetland's greater drought sensitivity. This macro-micro framework bridges long-term dynamics and process-scale hydrology, revealing that wetland evolution arises from the temporal alignment or misalignment of multiple drivers.
