AB  - Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and recurrent neural networks (RNNs) have adequately performed the hyperspectral image classification (HSIC) task. However, CNNs struggle to capture global features, and RNNs struggle to capture local features and long-range dependencies. This study proposes a novel HSIC method, called optimized multi-layer perceptron autoencoder bidirectional long short-term memory (MLP-AE-BiLSTM), to overcome these challenges. The proposed method integrates the MLP-AE to prevent overfitting and preserve spatial and spectral correlations, and the BiLSTM to model backward and forward spectral dependencies. First, the morphological operation is used to preprocess the HSI dataset, emphasizing the objects within it. We developed an Improved SIFT (I-SIFT) to capture more effective spatial features and utilize HOG and effective spectral feature extraction methods to obtain rich, fused spectral and spatial features. To improve the training efficiency and generalization, we developed a Bhattacharyya wingsuit flying search (BWFS) optimization to optimize the parameters of BiLSTM. The proposed optimized MLP-AE-BiLSTM model has been evaluated on the publicly available HACARUS dataset, achieving better accuracy (97.89%) than state-of-the-art methods. The source code for this study is available at https://github.com/RajatArya22/MLP-AE-BiLSTM.
