AB  - AbstractAccurate identification of unsound maize seeds is essential for ensuring grain storage safety and reliable quality assessment. Traditional seed identification methods typically rely on manual expertise and struggle to cope with the substantial variability and complexity of seed types. Although deep neural networks exhibit strong nonlinear feature extraction capabilities in hyperspectral imaging (HSI) classification, their performance in identifying unsound seeds is hindered by the high dimensionality of spectral data and the poor cross-source consistency. To overcome these challenges, we propose a Mamba-based model, termed CSSH-Mamba, which couples heterogeneous spectral-spatial features to enhance the identification accuracy of unsound maize seeds. In the spectral branch, the model incorporates a Self-adaptive Tensor-Regularization module (STM), which dynamically adjusts tensor rank while jointly modeling local consistency and global constraints to strengthen spectral feature representation. For the spatial branch, a Self-adaptive Grouped Dilated Convolution (SGDC) module is designed to capture multi-scale texture structures and compensate for the limitations of conventional convolutions in modeling spatial details. Subsequently, the model performs deep fusion of the heterogeneous spectral-spatial features and leverages the Mamba architecture to achieve efficient global semantic modeling, thereby generating more discriminative integrated feature representations. On the constructed SS-M−Dataset V1, CSSH-Mamba achieves 99.6% OA, 99.2% mAP and 99.4% Kappa, outperforming the second-best methods by 0.9%, 0.8%, and 0.6%, respectively. These results demonstrate the significant advantages and strong potential of the proposed model for precise grain seed identification.
