AB  - Fusing hyperspectral image (HSI) with auxiliary elevation information (AEI), such as light detection and ranging (LiDAR) or digital surface models (DSM), offers substantial potential for improving land cover classification performance. However, learning rich and discriminative features from each modality and effectively integrating them remains a challenging task in multimodal remote sensing image classification. Most existing graph convolutional network (GCN)-based multimodal methods rely on simple MLP feature projections and pairwise relational modeling, which limits their ability to capture the nonlinear and higher-order intra-modality dependencies required for effective multimodal learning. To address these limitations, we propose HCKNet, a multimodal fusion network that, for the first time, extends hypergraph convolutional networks (HGCNs) to multimodal classification scenarios. To further enhance feature learning, each modality branch incorporates Kolmogorov–Arnold Networks (KANs), and a multi-feature score-weighted fusion (MFSWF) module adaptively integrates predictions from both branches. Experimental results reveal that HCKNet significantly improves overall accuracy (OA), average accuracy (AA), and Kappa on three benchmark datasets, outperforming other state-of-the-art (SOTA) methods. The code used in this study is publicly available and can be accessed at the following GitHub repository:https://github.com/xsxah/HSI-classification/tree/master.
