AB  - In-situ sampling for deep-reservoir water quality monitoring is accurate but often impractical for routine, high-resolution vertical monitoring. Remote sensing (RS) provides an alternative for estimating multiple water quality variables (WQVs), including both optically active and non-optically active variables. However, most existing studies have focused on surface-level estimations, which are inadequate for deep reservoirs where WQVs’ profiles vary significantly with depth. While previous studies have compared remote sensing data sources for estimating water quality, this work addresses a critical gap by providing one of the first systematic evaluations of different data sources specifically for assessing WQVs at lower levels in deep water bodies. To achieve this, we introduce a novel multi-platform systematic framework that evaluates multiple RS data sources (including Sentinel-2 and Landsat 8/9 satellite imagery as well as unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) observations), alongside fused datasets and machine learning models to estimate vertical profiles of WQVs. The framework was applied to the Wadi Dayqah Dam (Oman), where seven WQVs (temperature, chlorophyll-a, turbidity, EC25, pH, oxidation–reduction potential, and dissolved oxygen) were modeled across depth zones using coincident in-situ measurements for training and validation. Results show that RS-based models can reproduce vertical WQV patterns beyond the surface layer, and that multi-source fusion improves estimation for several non-optically active variables compared with single-source models. Key contributions are: (1) a reproducible evaluation process for selecting RS sources and models for vertical profiling, (2) evidence that multi-source RS improves estimation of depth-varying WQVs in a deep reservoir, and (3) a demonstration on a large reservoir that the approach can extend operational RS monitoring below the surface. This framework can guide future vertical water-quality monitoring and multi-source RS integration.
