AB  - A critical generalization challenge limits the utility of conventional machine learning (ML) models for correcting satellite precipitation products (SPPs), particularly over complex terrain. Models relying exclusively on static geographic predictors often suffer from spatial overfitting, achieving deceptively high skill under standard temporal validation while failing to generalize to ungauged locations. To address this limitation, a synoptic-aware explainable artificial intelligence (SA-XAI) framework was developed, integrating dynamic atmospheric drivers from the ERA5 reanalysis with multiple satellite inputs to enforce physically consistent learning. Evaluated over a 20-year period (2004–2023) across the topographically complex central Iran, a rigorous spatial cross-validation (Spatial CV) exposed the illusory skill of static models. An ablation study identified the 200 hPa geopotential height (Z200) and 850 hPa meridional moisture flux (vq850) as highly effective and parsimonious physical drivers for this regional circulation setting. To capture the magnitude of high-impact events more effectively, the architecture was further upgraded to a two-stage Hurdle model enriched with deep thermodynamic constraints (e.g., convective available potential energy and total column water vapour). The framework demonstrated substantial operational value during hydrologically significant wet events (>5.0 mm day−1). Compared to a traditional quantile mapping (QM) baseline, the SA-XAI model improved the probability of detection (POD) by 41.4%. Furthermore, Shapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) was deployed as a rigorous internal diagnostic audit. The XAI analysis revealed an emergent synoptic importance shift, confirming that the model autonomously learns to dynamically prioritize large-scale synoptic forcing over error-prone satellite inputs during high-impact regional events. This framework bridges black-box ML and meteorological science, delivering the regionally transferable and reliable corrections essential for operational monitoring in data-scarce environments.
