AB  - Addressing the critical challenges in high-resolution remote sensing (RS) scenes, where complex geometric structures and environmental interference constrain the performance of existing hybrid architectures, and traditional paradigms struggle to balance fine-grained object extraction with complex background suppression due to the lack of physical perception dimensions, this paper proposes a multi-dimensional perception-driven network (EPDNet) for RS image semantic segmentation. First, the network leverages the complementary advantages of a heterogeneous dual-stream encoder based on CNN and Transformer to extract local fine textures and capture global semantics, upon which a synergistic mechanism of local direction and global context is designed. This mechanism introduces continuous directional perception based on Euler phase to explicitly constrain the geometric boundaries of anisotropic objects, and establishes macroscopic semantic continuity through hierarchical global attention, significantly enhancing the structural integrity. Second, to tackle boundary blurring caused by uneven illumination and shadows in RS scenarios, a Spatial-Frequency Synergistic Boundary Enhancement Mechanism (SFEM) is constructed to synergize the decoupling of environmental noise in the frequency domain with the sharpening of spatial gradients, improving the extraction precision of land cover boundaries under extreme lighting and shadow occlusion. Finally, a Hierarchical Contextual Aggregation Decoder (HCAD) is designed to deeply align heterogeneous features, utilizing a cross-scale spatial attention strategy to suppress large-scale background noise and adaptively refocus on high-saliency small targets, effectively mitigating the severe class imbalance in RS tasks. Extensive experiments on four public datasets, including Vaihingen and Potsdam, demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms comparative methods, verifying the effectiveness of the multi-dimensional perception-driven architecture.
