AB  - Accurate extraction of large-scale photovoltaic (PV) facilities from high-resolution satellite and Unmanned Aerial Vehicle (UAV) imagery is crucial for achieving global carbon neutrality. However, existing methods struggle to balance deep semantic representation with fine-grained edge localization amidst multi-scale morphological variations and complex background interferences. To overcome this limitation, this paper pioneers the introduction of the self-supervised vision foundation model (DINOv3) into remote sensing PV extraction by proposing PVDINO, a novel end-to-end framework. First, a DINOv3 Semantic Adaptation Encoder effectively adapts multi-level features to enhance deep global semantic representation. Second, an Edge Feature Mining Encoder introduces frequency-domain priors and flow field estimation for the fine-grained mining of shallow and deep features, respectively. Finally, a Cross-Guided Fusion Decoder synergizes these global semantic and edge-enhanced features for highly accurate extraction. Extensive experiments on the AIR-PV, PVP, and H-RPVS datasets demonstrate PVDINO's superiority. Notably, it yields an F1-score of 96.78% and an Intersection over Union (IoU) of 93.88% on the PVP dataset, outperforming state-of-the-art methods by up to 1.61% in IoU. Furthermore, PVDINO exhibits exceptional robustness in cross-scene generalization and large-scale mapping, providing a reliable automated monitoring solution for macro-energy infrastructure. The source code will be available at: https://github.com/whuzzzz/PVDINO.
