AB  - Solar photovoltaic (PV) power generation is a core clean energy source for addressing the energy crisis and climate change. Accurate monitoring of large-scale solar PV deployments is crucial. Existing work often reports on PV installation monitoring or growth assessments in developed regions, overlooking underdeveloped areas. This study leverages high-resolution bi-temporal satellite imagery to construct the first publicly available dataset for PV installation change detection, PVPanel-CD. This dataset contains bi-temporal remote sensing images covering representative African landscapes, along with pixel-level change annotations. We propose PVP-Adapter, a dual-branch spatial-frequency adaptive method for PV panel change detection, to achieve accurate monitoring of solar PV deployments across Africa. Specifically, we introduce a parameter-efficient adaptation scheme that fine-tunes frozen features from Vision Foundation Models (VFMs), facilitating knowledge transfer of parametric features and reducing dependence on a large number of training samples. Furthermore, we decouple the adaptive spatial features into low-frequency and high-frequency branches to perform differential change detection separately. To explore the transferability of the proposed PVP-Adapter, we also release six geographically diverse test domain datasets. We found that: (1) PVP-Adapter shows significant advantages in complex interference environments, surpassing existing change detection methods with an overall accuracy of 97.91%. (2) We use PVP-Adapter to calculate the growth of PV panel installations in Africa by 25.98 square kilometers from 2006 to 2024, and then discuss changes in environmental elements and the potential of solar energy in Africa. Our work makes it possible to automatically monitor changes in PV panel installations and human environment using bi-temporal RS images. The source code of the project is available at https://github.com/DonMuv/PVP-Adapter.
