AB  - Hyperspectral Change Detection, a core technology in remote sensing image processing, aims to accurately identify subtle land surface changes by leveraging the abundant spatial-spectral information of bi-temporal hyperspectral images. However, existing mainstream methods rely on CNNs or Transformers with fixed architectures, which not only lack efficient attention mechanisms to enhance the representation of differential features but also fail to fully explore the deep correlations between bi-temporal features due to their fixed convolution kernels and network structures. To address these issues, this paper proposes a novel Dual-branch Adaptive Dynamic Feature Enhancement Network (DADFENet), adaptively focusing on the spatial regions and spectral channels most relevant to change detection while suppressing irrelevant information. Its dual-branch architecture realizes an end-to-end mapping from bi-temporal images to change detection results, consisting of three blocks: the multiple Local-Global Feature Adaptive Enhancement (LGFAE) block, Difference Feature Learning Capability Enhancement (DFLCE) block, and Dual-branch Change Feature Dynamic Fusion (DCFDF) block. Specifically, the LGFAE block integrates Deformable Convolution (DC) and Adaptive Spatial-Spectral Hybrid Attention (AS2HA) to achieve cross-branch feature interaction and dynamic adjustment of spatial-spectral attention weights. The DFLCE block fuses DC, Deformable Spatial-Channel Attention (DSCA) and Wavelet Multiscale Attention (WMSA) to focus on core change regions and improve feature discriminability. The DCFDF block introduces a Spatial-Spectral Vision Transformer (SSViT) for global context modeling, realizes adaptive and efficient fusion of dual-branch features via a Dynamic Fusion (DF) module. Experimental results on four public hyperspectral datasets demonstrate that DADFENet outperforms state-of-the-art methods. The code will be open-sourced at https://github.com/SMingshuai/DADFENet.
