AB  - Accurate lithological mapping in semi-arid borate basins is important for exploration, yet it remains challenging where sedimentary and volcanic units exhibit similar spectral signatures. This study compares the performance of ASTER, Landsat 8 OLI, and Sentinel-2A data for lithological discrimination in the Emet–Hisarcık Borate Basin (western Türkiye). Support Vector Machine (SVM) and Maximum Likelihood Classification (MLC) were implemented using both the original spectral bands and feature sets derived from Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and Minimum Noise Fraction (MNF) transforms. Classification results were assessed using 101 independent validation points. In all tested cases, SVM yielded higher accuracies than MLC. The highest performance was obtained with the ASTER–PCA–SVM approach (Overall Accuracy = 93%; κ = 0.92). This result likely reflects ASTER’s denser sampling in the SWIR region, which enhances the separation of carbonate- and OH-bearing lithologies. Sentinel-2A also produced high accuracy (OA = 89%), whereas Landsat 8 OLI showed the lowest performance (OA ≈ 71–72%). Overall, the findings indicate that combining ASTER SWIR information with PCA and SVM provides a practical and transferable solution for lithological mapping in complex semi-arid settings.
