AB  - Monitoring water quality in reservoirs is critical for municipal water security and agricultural viability. UAV imagery has been widely applied to monitor the spatiotemporal variation of water quality parameters (WQPs) in many inland waters. However, its application over large, featureless aquatic environments has been constrained due to the inherent limitations of conventional photogrammetry in georeferencing imagery. Furthermore, although numerous studies have leveraged multispectral UAV imagery for WQP estimation, the potential of integrating UAV-acquired thermal infrared data to enhance predictive performance remains largely underexplored. In this regard, we adapted a framework for preprocessing and direct georeferencing of UAV imagery over the Wadi Dayqah reservoir, Oman’s largest dam. Six WQPs, namely chlorophyll-a (Chl-a), dissolved oxygen (DO), temperature, electrical conductivity (EC), turbidity, and oxidation–reduction potential (ORP), were estimated using four machine learning models (LR, SVR, RF, XGBoost) and three tabular deep learning algorithms (TabPFN, TabNet, FT-Transformer). Two scenarios were used to estimate WQPs. In the first, multispectral imagery was used, whereas in the second, multispectral data was combined with thermal infrared data. Results showed that TabPFN and TabNet outperformed machine learning algorithms. Additionally, incorporating thermal imagery enhanced the accuracy of WQP estimation, with the best improvements observed for temperature (TabPFN, from R2 = 0.93 to 0.99), DO (TabNet, from R2 = 0.71 to 0.79), and turbidity (TabPFN, from R2 = 0.83 to 0.90), whereas Chl-a estimation showed minor improvements.
