AB  - Flood inundation monitoring using satellite data plays a crucial role in emergency response. To harness the advantages of Multispectral (MS) and Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) sensors while mitigating their limitations, an integration of multi-source satellite imagery is crucial. In this study, we introduced U-Net to extract water bodies from scenes captured by Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 satellites. The performance of the U-Net model was evaluated using both a standard dataset and real-world cases. Different combinations of multi-source satellite imagery were then compared to investigate the variations across images and identify the most effective inputs. Finally, an explainable framework called Grad-CAM was employed to gain insights into the active regions within the deep learning models’ inner layers. The U-Net model achieved strong segmentation performance on Sentinel-1 imagery, with an overall accuracy of 0.95 and an intersection over union (IoU) of 0.33. The model produced a 1.41% false-water rate and a 34.62% false-dry rate, both outperforming the baseline. When Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 data were jointly used as inputs, overall accuracy increased to 0.97 and IoU increased to 0.33, while the false-positive and false-negative rates decreased to 0.86% and 27.85% respectively, surpassing models trained on either data source alone. Additionally, the incorporation of auxiliary information such as DEM further enhanced the model’s performance. The analysis of the shallow layers revealed their remarkable transparency and emphasized the necessity of introducing deep learning architecture in multi-source satellite imagery applications. This study can help enhance the utilization efficiency of multi-source satellite data and improve the understanding of deep learning models used for monitoring flood inundation.
