AB  - Black-box algorithms, such as machine learning and deep learning, dominate soybean remote sensing mapping, their opaque decision-making processes often obscure valuable insights. The rise of Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI) has highlighted the importance of interpretability in such methods. However, traditional knowledge-based approaches for soybean mapping rely heavily on extensive experimentation and suffer from slow knowledge updates. To bridge this gap, this study introduces a novel knowledge extraction technique that interprets trained RF models, integrates the insights into the ensemble process to reconstruct decision rules, and selects the most effective rules as the target. Utilizing Sentinel-1/2 satellite imagery, 31 features were generated, and an interpretable soybean mapping approach (ISMA) was developed, comprising four key feature rules: B11 > 0.194, EVI > 0.811, GNDVI > 0.755, and NDre > 0.602. The evaluation revealed that ISMA achieved accuracy levels between 89.47% and 96.02%, with F1-scores ranging from 76.52% to 89.01%, demonstrating robust classification performance. Although ISMA’s extraction accuracy was marginally lower than that of the original RF model, it utilized only four features, reducing data volume by 87.1% while maintaining high interpretability. ISMA represents a viable alternative to black-box algorithms in the era of XAI, offering a balance between precision, interpretability, and efficiency.
