AB  - Accurate estimation of aboveground biomass (AGB) in mountainous landscapes remains challenging because of severe speckle contamination in Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) data and complex terrain-induced scattering mechanisms. The current study presents a novel framework integrating advanced speckle filtering, Decision-Making Trial and Evaluation Laboratory (DEMETEL), and Innovative Trend Analysis (ITA) to enhance biomass retrieval over the Kumbhalgarh Wildlife Sanctuary (KWLS). Multi-sensor data, including Sentinel-1C-band SAR, ALOS-2 PALSAR-2 L-band SAR, Sentinel-2 optical imagery, Landsat-9 surface reflectance, and GEDI LiDAR footprints were employed. Eight speckle filters (Boxcar, Lee, Refined Lee, IDAN, Frost, Median, Gamma, and Lee Sigma) were evaluated systematically. DEMETEL was introduced in SAR filtering to establish causal relationships among filters and objectively rank their influence on biomass prediction. Furthermore, ITA was applied to validate monotonic and non-linear trends between filtered backscatter and field-measured canopy height, overcoming the limitations of conventional correlation analysis. Machine learning models (Random Forest, XGBoost, and Multi-Layer Perceptron [MLP]) were trained using multi-sensor predictors. The results showed that the IDAN and Gamma filters consistently emerged as dominant causal drivers, improving the model performance (R2 up to 0.93, RMSE = 11.37 Mg/ha). The MLP model outperformed XGBoost and Random Forest in this heterogeneous landscape. Spatial biomass maps revealed a strong correspondence between terrain gradients and vegetation density. This study demonstrated that the DEMETEL and ITA significantly advanced SAR-based biomass modelling in heterogeneous mountainous environments, offering a transferable framework for carbon monitoring and forest management.
