AB  - Improving the accuracy of land surface temperature (LST) retrieval from thermal infrared (TIR) remote sensing and inverting the properties of subsurface geothermal reservoirs remain significant challenges. This has particularly constrained the integration of TIR remote sensing with other exploration methodologies, such as geochemical surveys. To address this issue, we derived thermal infrared information from Landsat 8 and MODIS data, with Sentinel-2 and SRTM data providing auxiliary optical and topographic features in the Yingshan geothermal field. Subsequently, we adopted the eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) machine learning model to perform spatial downscaling of LST. By establishing the optimal feature combination for temperature retrieval, we achieved high-precision surface temperature inversion and compared its performance with the traditional bilinear interpolation and DisTrad models. In parallel, we developed a processing workflow for altered mineral identification based on ASTER and Landsat 8 reflectance data. Finally, we conducted a comprehensive analysis integrating the downscaled LST, thermally altered minerals, and in-situ geochemical data including soil gas Rn, Hg, and geothermal gradient (GTG), to improve the accuracy of geothermal prospect targeting. The results show that the XGBoost model significantly enhanced the detail richness of the LST map, increasing the entropy value from 3.13 to 3.30, which outperforms the results of bilinear interpolation (3.12) and the DisTrad model (3.14). Validation against in-situ surface temperature measurements shows that the XGBoost model achieved a mean bias error (MBE) of -0.492 ± 0.2 °C and a root mean square error (RMSE) of 1.39 ± 0.0702 °C, which are significantly better than the results of the DisTrad model (MBE = 0.539 ± 0.2 °C; RMSE = 2.77 ± 0.0387 °C) and bilinear interpolation (MBE = 0.625 ± 0.2 °C; RMSE = 2.98 ± 0.0418 °C). Furthermore, the P-P plot analysis indicates that the XGBoost model significantly improved the identification accuracy for both the relatively high-temperature geothermal fields and the low-temperature vegetation-covered areas. Our TIR remote sensing analysis identified four types of hydrothermal alteration: propylitic, argillization, biotitization, and sericitization, all of which were validated by field investigations. In the focal research area, we identified five high-temperature anomalies, along with their corresponding thermally altered mineral anomalies. The results indicate that the geothermal reservoirs in the anomaly zones are dominated by medium-acidic and medium-low temperature environments. Moreover, through comprehensive integration with soil gas Rn, Hg, and geothermal gradient (GTG), we classified the thermal anomalies into three distinct types with complete quantitative signatures: (1) Type 1 (geothermal fields): showed LST anomalies of 23.3 ± 1.12–24.6 ± 1.22 °C, with the presence of surface hydrothermal alteration minerals, paired with elevated Hg and GTG; (2) Type 2 (hidden faults): had normal LST, with no detectable hydrothermal alteration minerals, but pronounced Rn anomalies; (3) Type 3 (heat island/conductive zones): exhibited extreme LST fluctuations, with no detectable hydrothermal alteration minerals, paired with elevated GTGs but weak geochemical anomalies. This classification result suggests that downscaled LST, when integrated with soil gas Rn, Hg, geothermal gradient, and structural information, can assist in distinguishing known geothermal fields from potential concealed structural or conductive zones. The XGBoost-based downscaling method, combined with hydrothermal altered mineral identification, can significantly improve the accuracy of geothermal exploration targeting, analyze the genesis of geothermal distribution at the initial exploration stage, and substantially reduce the cost of geothermal system exploration.
