AB  - Maize is one of the most important food crops worldwide, and precise management of its growth cycle is crucial for ensuring food security. The phenological stages of maize can be influenced by geographical location, climatic variations, and field management practices. Timely and accurate identification of maize phenological stages is vital for optimizing agricultural management, reducing fertilizer input, and improving yield prediction accuracy. However, current methods for maize phenological identification not only exhibit poor generalizability across spatiotemporal scales but also require intensive data inputs, further exacerbating prediction delays. In this study, we propose a new approach for the real-time mapping of maize phenological information by integrating time information obtained from remote sensing index and meteorological and spectral reflectance (Ref) data. We found the date when the Green–Red Vegetation Index (GRVI) equals zero (DOYGRVI=0) occurs near the jointing stage. At this stage, the cumulative meteorological and physical variables of maize growth are strongly associated with subsequent light and heat conditions. Based on this time information, we incorporated cumulative meteorological data precipitation, accumulated growing degree days, accumulated average radiative power density (AARPD), and data such as RED, GREEN, NIR, and SWIR Ref values corresponding to the DOYGRVI=0 as machine learning model inputs to predict the difference between the AARPD at the predicted phenological date and the AARPD corresponding to DOYGRVI=0 (∆AARPD[jls-end-space/]). The ∆AARPD[jls-end-space/]prediction process involves separately conducting model training and prediction within distinct DOYGRVI=0 ranges. Subsequently, the prediction outputs are integrated with daily radiative power density data to enable real-time phenological stage forecasting. And this set of strategies demonstrated robust transferability: when trained on U.S. maize-growing regions, it achieved 83% phenological identification accuracy (RMSE <2.5 days). Without recalibration, it remained directly applicable to Jilin, China—a key maize-producing area—exhibiting increased yet operationally acceptable errors (RMSE: 6.5 days). Compared to existing phenological extraction methods that rely on full-season remote sensing time series, our framework requires satellite imagery only around the early growth stage, substantially reducing image input demands. Moreover, the model demonstrated robust cross-regional transferability: when trained on U.S. data and directly applied to Jilin, China, without recalibration, it maintained operationally acceptable accuracy (RMSE: 6.5 days). This enables cost-effective real-time monitoring and demonstrates significant deployment potential across global maize belts, particularly in data-scarce regions.
