AB  - The morphology of the active layer in organic photovoltaic materials has a significant impact on device performance. As a representative donor–acceptor blend system, the aggregate structure of P3HT:PCBM is widely recognized as playing a crucial role in the photoelectric conversion process; however, its underlying mechanism remains to be systematically elucidated. To establish a quantitative relationship between aggregate morphology and device performance, this study proposes an analytical framework that integrates hyperspectral imaging and deep neural networks. First, hyperspectral technology is employed to characterize the aggregates and construct a corresponding hyperspectral image dataset. Subsequently, an improved UNet model is used for image segmentation, while YOLO V10 is applied for object detection to extract key morphological features, such as surface density and number density. To address the limitations of conventional networks in microstructural recognition, a novel texture-Mamba attention module is designed and incorporated into the backbone to enhance both recognition accuracy and model generalization. Finally, statistical analysis is conducted to systematically evaluate the correlations between aggregate morphological features and device performance parameters. Experimental results show that, in in-distribution tests, the improved model achieves an accuracy of 99.39 % and a mean average precision of 94.3 %. Further evaluations on two out-of-distribution datasets, derived from experimental data collection and literature mining, demonstrate that aggregate morphological features exhibit strong negative correlations with key electrical parameters (Jsc, FF, and PCE) and a strong positive correlation with Voc. The correlation coefficients between surface density and these parameters are −0.99, −0.92, −0.99, and 0.97, respectively. These correlations are slightly stronger than those associated with number density, indicating that surface density may play a more decisive role in determining device performance.
