AB  - Deep learning models’ generalization ability is severely hampered in cross-scene hyperspectral image (HSI) classification due to domain shift. Although domain generalization (DG) is a promising solution, current HSI DG methods often suffer from inadequate alignment. They fail to fully mitigate domain shift due to augmentation alignment using complex generation, insufficient semantic exploitation, and untapped pretrained knowledge from foundation models. To mitigate this gap, we propose a multilevel alignment framework for HSI DG, integrating three complementary alignment mechanisms built around a data-semantic-knowledge guiding paradigm. Specifically, at the data level, augmentation alignment uses a residual-separated strategy to separate the residual components of the original and augmented samples. It then maximizes the residual entropy to make the feature extractor and classifier more robust to domain-specific changes. At the semantic level, cross-modal semantic alignment uses a three-tier textual framework including coarse-grained class-based texts made from templates, fine-grained class-based attributes made by large language models, and instance-based descriptions made by encoding the relationships between pixel positions. These comprehensive text features are then fused and aligned with HSI features. At the knowledge level, pretrained knowledge alignment employs an adaptive weight fusion strategy to align HSI features with pretrained features from foundation models, facilitating efficient knowledge transfer. Experiments on three public cross-scene HSI datasets show the proposed framework outperforms comparison DG methods, validating the effectiveness of multilevel alignment in improving cross-DG.
ER  -

