AB  - Extreme weather events that can take place are such as hurricanes, floods, and heatwaves. These extreme weather events can have a catastrophically adverse impact on human lives, infrastructure, and ecosystems, making it imperative to have robust prediction and detection mechanisms in place. Thus, this paper seeks to unlock the very essence of deep learning techniques for the detection and prediction of extreme weather events through the application of multidimensional meteorological datasets. The study exploits advanced neural network architectures like Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) and Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) to combine the spatial and time dimension the data for enhancing the accuracy of predictions. The method used in this study included feature extraction from satellite data, sensor data, and the history of weather patterns in conjunction with the real-time projection of events. A case study comparing the model with traditional statistical procedures shows that the proposed method could decrease the amount of false positives detected and increase the lead time of warnings. The study concludes that deep learning could be a game-changer in meteorology with the potential to deliver pragmatic solutions for disaster preparedness and strategies for climate resilience through its insights.
