AB  - This research investigates the application of machine learning-based regression models to estimate tissue perfusion parameters from multispectral imaging data. Our purpose is to establish a robust method for predicting the changes in deoxygenated and oxygenated hemoglobin at the fingertip using spectral unmixing algorithms. Multispectral images are processed to extract elementary spectral profiles or end-members and their fractional concentrations or abundances maps, providing a detailed analysis of hemoglobin distribution. The evaluation focuses on the relationship between the extracted abundances and changes in hemoglobin by using metrics, such as normalized reconstruction error and the adjusted coefficient of determination to quantify performance. As reference for validation, the estimated changes in deoxygenated and oxygenated hemoglobin, obtained by photoplethysmography, are compared with our proposal. In addition, perfusion maps representing the spatial distribution of changes in hemoglobin at the fingertip are generated from the proposed approach. The generalization capability of the regression models is further confirmed using crossvalidation, ensuring the robustness of the proposed approach across varying datasets. The most prominent results show a normalized reconstruction error of 0.009 ± 0.010 , and adjusted coefficient of determination of 0.988 ± 0.011 using linear spectral unmixing and a random forest regressor. Tissue perfusion maps provide a detailed visual representation of the changes in deoxygenated and oxygenated hemoglobin on the fingertip, marking a significant advancement in perfusion monitoring.
