AB  - The paper presents a novel LegalTech-driven framework for assessing corporate carbon footprints and their consequential impact on public health through integrated legal, environmental, and epidemiological data analytics. The system synthesizes data from diverse sources-ESG disclosures, satellite-based Sentinel-5P imagery, IoT-enabled sensors, and health databases (CDC, WHO, NDHM)-to compute emissions, evaluate legal compliance, and predict health risks. A Transformer-based LegalBERT model extracts regulatory thresholds and statutory limits, which are aligned with emissions using a custom-built Legal Compliance Alignment Module (LCAM). A GeoHealth Linkage Engine (GLE) overlays emissions with disease incidence through weighted geospatial indexing. A Legal-Augmented Graph Neural Network (LA-GNN) infers causality between corporate emissions and health outcomes while accounting for legal parameters. Additionally, a sentiment analysis module captures public and legal discourse using a Multi-Head Cross-Attention BERT-GRU hybrid. The Dynamic Legal Compliance Score (DLCS) integrates real-time emissions, legal violations, and sentiment to rank firms into risk categories. The proposed model achieved an overall accuracy of 93.21% in legal compliance classification and 91.56% causality prediction for health impact detection. Experimental results show strong correlations between emission zones and respiratory disease prevalence, reinforcing the validity of the framework. The interdisciplinary architecture provides regulators, environmental agencies, and policymakers with a robust decision-support system for proactive climate governance. By combining Legal AI, deep learning, and geospatial health mapping, the framework represents a significant advancement in environmental public health accountability and compliance automation.
