AB  - This study presents a hybrid deep learning model for multiscale disaster feature extraction and temporal change detection utilizing multitemporal remote sensing pictures, including SAR, optical, and multispectral imagery. This model surpasses current alternatives by employing multiscale feature extraction, superpixel segmentation, attention-aggregating convolutional neural networks, and a Siamese transformer-based approach for temporal change detection. The model employs quadrant-based data pre-processing, spatial attention and saliency units, and multi-regularization post-processing with SVM to enhance classification accuracy. The technique was tested on tasks such as flood extent mapping, building damage categorization, water body extraction, and shoreline delimitation. The experimental results demonstrate that our model outperformed existing state-of-the-art models, including STFCD, CGSANET, TGCFeWD, and conventional SVM-based classifiers across all evaluated issues. Specifically, our approach attained a mean accuracy of 95.2%, an F1-measure of 91.1%, and a 5-10% improvement in state-of-the-art baseline Intersection over Union (IoU) across several crisis situations and datasets. The findings validate the efficacy of employing attention-based deep learning combined with spatiotemporal data fusion for autonomous and accurate disaster monitoring. The framework's modality job and methodological robustness afford it excellent potential for practical implementation in operational infrastructure resilience planning, environmental monitoring, and disaster response.
