AB  - The proposed work describes a large-scale, interactive Google Earth Engine application for multi-temporal Land Use/Land Cover study. Furthermore, the study investigates the utilization of multi-sensor satellite imaging data from Landsat 5, Landsat 7, Landsat 8, and Sentinel-2 satellites to produce a consistent one-year composite from 1995 to 2025. To provide accurate results for LULC classification, sophisticated pre-processing, cloud mask layer operations, spectral index calculations (NDVI, EVI, NDBI, MNDWI, BSI, UI), and supervised machine learning classifiers such as Random Forest, SVM, and CART are utilized. The aim of the tool is to ensure that training models have a graphical interface through which accuracy checking, time series analysis, change detection, investigation of patterns, and graphics production are achieved. The tool may be applied to compute area statistics concerning class, explore temporal changes, analyze class transitions, and illustrate vegetation dynamics. Pixel-level exploration of classified raster layers, vector conversion, and production of time-lapse videos are further capabilities offered. In conclusion, the platform offered is a complete, scaleable, and easy-to-use solution designed for evaluation and sustained LULC change detection.
