AB  - The southwest region of China is mostly hilly and mountainous, and it is very easy for a large number of landslides triggered by earthquakes or heavy rains, resulting in casualties, economic losses, and environmental damage. This paper proposes a deep learning-based multi-feature correlation and fusion detection method to meet the needs of remote sensing identification of targets and rapid extraction of disaster situations after large-scale mass landslides occur. Firstly, the remote sensing images before and after the landslide were preprocessed and transformed into surface reflectance images with the same coordinate system and spatial resolution. Secondly, the surface reflectance images are correlated pixel by pixel in spectral dimension and combined to form a data cube that integrates the spatial, spectral, and temporal characteristics of the landslide and typical background. Thirdly, a deep learning-based landslide remote sensing detection model is constructed based on the long and short-term memory network, which is with potential advantage in time-varying information capture, for mining and extracting multi-dimensional feature information of background in multispectral remote sensing images. Finally, the river valley of Moxi Town, which is about 10 km away from the epicenter of the Luding 6.8 magnitude earthquake, was selected as the study area, and a comparative experiment of landslides and typical background detection was carried out based on multispectral remote sensing data from Sentinel-2 satellites. The experimental results show that, compared with the popular convolutional neural network (CNN) model and the recurrent neural network (RNN) model commonly used in time series analysis, the proposed model in this paper can more effectively utilize the spectral reflectance information and temporal change information of background in time-series remote sensing images, and has better accuracy in the detection of landslides and typical background features in remote sensing images than the other two models, and the detection results can maintain high consistency with the true distribution of background in the study area.
