AB  - Rivers are integral components of ecosystems, and monitoring nitrogen and phosphorus is critical for effective riverine water quality management. Although satellite remote sensing has been widely employed for large-scale monitoring of lakes and coastal zones, narrow river reaches occupy only a few pixels in satellite images, severely limiting its applicability. Moreover, the weak spectral responses of nitrogen and phosphorus constrain the accuracy of traditional empirical and machine learning retrieval methods. Existing deep learning approaches often overlook the extraction of essential spatial context around sampling locations, resulting in limited model generalizability and predictive performance. In this study, we introduced the Important Spatial Features Extraction Network (ISFE-Net), which leverages high spatial resolution UAV hyperspectral imagery to retrieve water quality parameters across the full Juzhang river basin as well as its upper, middle, and lower reaches. The ISFE-Net introduced a spatial feature extraction module that identifies the most important image information and filters out irrelevant noise, thereby substantially enhancing retrieval accuracy. On the testing set, ISFE-Net achieved R2 values of 0.689, 0.614, and 0.799 for NH3 -N, TN, and TP across the entire basin. The model achieved its best performance in the middle and lower reaches, where NH3 -N, TN, and TP yielded testing R2 values of 0.785, 0.787, and 0.829, with RMSEs of 0.006, 0.165, and 0.003 mg/L and MAPEs of 11.7 %, 9.2 %, and 6.9 %, respectively. These results demonstrated ISFE-Net’s strong generalizability across varied riverine environments and offer a new approach for high precision remote sensing retrieval of non-optically active water quality parameters.
