AB  - This study integrates hyperspectral remote sensing (HRS) and machine learning (ML) techniques for assessing soil heavy metal contamination in the Zeïda mining area of Morocco, a region with a history of significant mining activity, particularly lead extraction. The study focuses on six potentially toxic elements (PTEs)—copper (Cu), chromium (Cr), manganese (Mn), cadmium (Cd), lead (Pb), and zinc (Zn)—which are prevalent in the area due to mining operations. A total of 77 soil samples were collected across various zones of the mining area, and spectral data were obtained using an ASD FieldSpec® 3 spectroradiometer. To enhance the spectral feature sensitivity, five spectral transformations were applied, with the second derivative (SD) and asymmetric second derivative (ASD) proving the most effective in improving correlations with heavy metal concentrations. Machine learning models, including Sparse Multinomial Logistic Regression (SMLR), Regression, and Backpropagation (BP), were utilized to develop inversion models for predicting the concentration of the studied metals. Among these models, the Regression model using ASD-transformed spectra showed the highest performance, with R2 values of 0.827 for Cu, 0.921 for Cr, 0.998 for Mn, and strong results for Pb, Cd, and Zn as well. These models exhibited low root mean square errors (RMSE), indicating reliable predictive power. The study demonstrates the utility of HRS and ML for large-scale, non-invasive environmental monitoring, offering a practical framework for mapping and assessing the spatial distribution of heavy metal pollution in mining-impacted regions. The findings contribute to environmental risk assessment and provide valuable information for soil remediation planning in areas with historical and ongoing mining activities.
