AB  - Accurate estimation of forest area at the national scale is a challenging task in environmentally heterogeneous, datascarce regions. This study presents a machine learning- based approach for forest area mapping across the entire ter- ritory of Kazakhstan using multi-source satellite imagery. The methodology integrates optical Sentinel-2 multispectral data with Sentinel-1 synthetic aperture radar backscatter features. A feature stack comprising spectral bands, vegetation indices, temporal statistics, and radar-derived metrics was constructed and resampled to an 80 m spatial grid. Supervised forest classification was performed using a gradient-boosting decision tree model trained on spatially grouped reference samples to reduce spatial autocorrelation. All data preprocessing and feature extraction were carried out in Google Earth Engine, while model training and large- scale inference were implemented in Google Colab using a block- wise processing strategy. This design enabled memory-efficient nationwide inference without tiling artifacts. The resulting binary forest map corresponds to satellite observations acquired during 2021-2022. Quantitative analysis of the produced forest mask indicates a total forest area of approximately 2.14 million hectares. The results demonstrate that the proposed approach provides a scalable and reproducible framework for national forest monitoring, serving as a basis for future assessments of forest dynamics in Kazakhstan.
