AB  - Semisupervised change detection (CD) in remote sensing images faces a challenge wherein existing methods employ uniform processing strategies that fail to account for spatial heterogeneity in prediction difficulty, resulting in insufficient learning of difficult regions such as change boundaries and subtle variations that exhibit high uncertainty yet contain crucial discriminative information for accurate detection. To systematically address this limitation, this article proposes uncertainty-guided semisupervised CD (USCD), a novel framework that introduces a triple-guarantee mechanism for targeted learning reinforcement in difficult regions based on systematic uncertainty quantification. The framework comprises three synergistic components: uncertainty-aware protective augmentation (UAPA) employs window-based difficulty assessment to identify high-uncertainty regions and implements selective copy-paste augmentation that preserves critical information while maintaining data diversity; difficult region contrastive learning (DRCL) enhances feature discriminability in challenging areas through both local contrastive learning with anchor-based hard sample mining and global contrastive learning using prototype-based memory banks; and uncertainty-guided loss reweighting (UGLR) applies exponential adaptive weighting that strengthens supervision signals in high-uncertainty labeled regions while simultaneously suppressing noise propagation from unreliable pseudolabels in unlabeled data. Extensive experimental validation across six diverse benchmark datasets, including LEVIR-CD, WHU-CD, CDD, S2Looking, SYSU-CD, and JL1-CD, demonstrates that USCD achieves state-of-the-art performance with limited labeled data, showing substantial improvements, particularly concentrated in high-uncertainty regions, robust generalization capability validated through cross-domain transfer experiments, and competitive computational efficiency comparable to existing methods. Code is publicly available at https://github.com/G124556/USCD.
