AB  - Hyperspectral remote sensing has been widely used in geosciences due to its ability to extract mineralogical information from rock surfaces by recording their reflected light in hundreds of high-resolution spectral bands. In this study, a lithological classification of an outcrop is performed using a hyperspectral image (HSI) captured in the Short-Wave Infrared region. To implement this classification, a novel deep neural network architecture based on Transformer model and a sequential combination of Fourier Neural Operators and channel-wise self-attention layers is used, which allows the combination of benefits from analyzing the image in both spatial and frequency domains. Before processing, a principal component analysis (PCA) was performed to reduce the dimensionality of the image. The classification model achieved an average test performance of 88% accuracy and 87% F1-score, with peak values reaching 91% for both metrics.
