AB  - This study presents a novel approach to hyperspectral mineral classification by leveraging interpretable neural networks trained on spectral libraries for real-world mineral mapping. Using single-class and multi-class neural networks, we analyze a hyperspectral image of Cuprite, Nevada, with a focus on alunite-rich regions. The single-class model, trained on a pure-mineral dataset, achieves an accuracy of 87.72% and effectively distinguishes alunite-rich areas from their surroundings. The multi-class model, designed to unmix spectra into mineral components, provides insights into mineral distributions despite lower quantitative accuracy (6.36%) due to spectral noise and overlap. These findings demonstrate the potential of interpretable models trained on library data to analyze complex field images, addressing challenges in hyperspectral imaging for geological exploration.
