AB  - Hyperspectral image classification is a prominent technique due to the volume of spatial and spectral data present in these images. However, achieving high accuracy often results in robust but computationally complex machine learning models, or lightweight models that sacrifice accuracy. Given this, we propose a lighter, hardware-friendly yet effective classification architecture by integrating a segmentation stage into the spatial data processing, aiming to enhance accuracy without significantly increasing computational costs. The solution fuses the output of the Superpixels Extracted via the Energy-Driven Sampling (SEEDS) segmentation algorithm with the output data from a convolutional layer step of a 1D Convolutional Neural Network (1D CNN) architecture to enhance classification accuracy. The results showed a 10% improvement in overall metrics and up to a 44% increase in class-specific metrics by integrating segmentation into the one-dimensional architecture.
ER  -

