AB  - Tea is a commercially important plantation crop in India and requires reliable monitoring to support sustainable management and production. Accurate spatial delineation of tea plantations is essential for agricultural planning, land management, and tea area estimation in complex highland terrains. However, mapping tea plantations using optical satellite data is challenging because of the spectral similarity between tea plantations, trees, and grasslands. Many existing studies rely on the manual extraction of spectral, spatial, or phenological features from optical satellite images, and their performance is significantly affected by cloud cover, irregular data availability, and the limited spatial and textural information available in moderate-resolution imagery. At this spatial resolution, individual tea rows and tea plantations intermixed with trees often appear as homogeneous green patches that are difficult to distinguish from dense natural vegetation. These limitations reduce the reliability of such approaches for mapping large-area tea plantations. In this study, a novel framework that combines Google Satellite Embeddings (GSE) with a Squeeze-and-Excitation attention-based U-Net (SE-U-Net) is proposed for tea plantation mapping. The compact spectral, spatial, and temporal representations provided by GSE enable the model to learn discriminative tea plantation characteristics more effectively. In addition, a tea-focused imbalance-handling strategy based on tea-aware patch filtering, selective oversampling, spatial augmentation, and a hybrid loss function is incorporated to improve model training. For comparison, experiments are also conducted using single-date Sentinel-2 imagery and Sentinel-2 NDVI time-series data. The experimental results on the polygon-based reference dataset show that the GSE-based SE-U-Net achieved the highest performance, with an overall accuracy of 98.50%, compared to other input data types and classification models. Cross-district evaluation further demonstrated promising transferability, achieving overall accuracies of 93.75% and 83.48% on two independent tea-growing regions, indicating the potential of the proposed framework for large-area tea plantation mapping.
ER  -

