AB  - Accurate identification and spatial mapping of hydrated minerals on Mars are fundamental for constraining ancient aqueous activity and reconstructing Martian environmental evolution. However, mineral recognition from orbital hyperspectral data remains challenging due to striping noise, weak diagnostic absorptions, complex background mixing, and limited labeled samples, which hinder classification accuracy and cross-scene generalization. To address these challenges, we propose a Vision Mamba with spectral attention (VMSA) framework for hydrated-mineral classification and mapping from Compact Reconnaissance Imaging Spectrometer for Mars (CRISM) hyperspectral data. VMSA adopts a coupled dual-branch architecture, in which a contextual spectral–spatial branch (CSB) learns stable spectral–spatial representations and a diagnostic-prior branch (DPB) explicitly models mineral-sensitive absorption priors, followed by joint discrimination through a prior-guided fusion (PGF) module. Based on public CRISM data resources, we further construct a unified hydrated-mineral benchmark and systematically evaluate the proposed method on three standardized subbenchmarks, namely, phyllosilicates, sulfates, and carbonates. Experimental results on the unified CRISM hydrated-mineral benchmark show that VMSA achieves strong and class-balanced performance across the phyllosilicate, sulfate, and carbonate subbenchmarks under a consistent evaluation protocol. In addition to benchmark-level quantitative comparison, we further conduct an endpoint-mined controlled input-space stress test to examine the behavior of the model under reduced hard-pair separability and diagnostic-prior perturbation. The results indicate that the diagnostic-prior pathway and PGF help to preserve target-versus-competing class margins when contextual/background cues become increasingly ambiguous. External evaluations on public hyperspectral image classification benchmarks, including Pavia University, Indian Pines, and Salinas, further support the transferability of the spectral–spatial backbone, while the CRISM-specific DPB remains dedicated to hydrated-mineral recognition. Finally, ratioed CRISM benchmark spectra were analyzed jointly with Mineral Identification and Classification Algorithm (MICA)-corrected reference spectra and laboratory spectra, while continuum-normalized band-depth contrasts were quantified for representative hard mineral pairs to assess the spectral discriminability and mineralogical consistency of VMSA predictions. These results suggest that VMSA provides a physically informed spectral–spatial framework for automated hydrated-mineral classification and mapping from CRISM data.
