AB  - Rapid and timely identification of surface coal mining disturbances is important for mining governance and environmental sustainability analyses. However, automated methods for detecting mining disturbances remain inadequate because current studies require manual tasks, such as sample training or multiparameter settings, when they are applied to a new region. This article proposes an automated method for identifying mining disturbances using the Landsat time series. The method automatically and adaptively generates normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI) templates for each vegetation change trajectory according to the typical NDVI pattern in surface mining regions. Each pixel is subsequently classified as a specific vegetation change trajectory using the NDVI templates as training samples and the dynamic time warping (DTW)-based K-nearest neighbor (KNN) function as the classifier. The potential mining disturbance is extracted on the basis of the classification results of the vegetation change trajectory. Mining disturbances are identified using the topological relationship between the exposed coal generated by a spectral index and the potential mining disturbance. As no manual task is required in the sample generation and the classification task, the identification process is fully automated after data preprocessing. When tested in five globally distributed study areas featuring diverse vegetation types, the method shows high-level performance in identifying mining disturbances, disturbance years, and recovery years (±1 year), achieving accuracies of no less than 94.4%, 82.9%, and 73.3%, respectively. Given the high degree of automation and freely available Landsat imagery, the method is transferable to other regions.
