AB  - Staple foods play a vital role in human nutrition. Corn, rice, wheat, and tubes account for 90 % of the global staple food consumption, continuously providing essential nutrients such as starch, protein, minerals, and vitamins. This study reviews the multi-dimensional nondestructive detection of staple food quality and safety using machine learning-based visible (VIS)/near-infrared (NIR) spectroscopy and hyperspectral imaging (HSI). Staple food attributes are primarily assessed based on sensory, chemical compositions, internal defects, mycotoxins, and adulteration. HSI technology exhibits significant advantages in extracting food features, enabling more efficient and comprehensive acquisition of sample information. In terms of algorithm application, Support Vector Machine (SVM) and Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) demonstrate broad applicability. SVM has a relatively high application frequency in mycotoxin detection tasks, while CNN demonstrates excellent performance across multiple domains including sensory feature analysis, chemical composition detection, internal defect identification, and mycotoxin detection. In addition, Partial Least Squares Regression (PLSR) and Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA) have become the mainstream algorithms of choice for internal defect detection due to their unique advantages.
