AB  - This study analyzes the spatio-temporal dynamics of mangrove cover in Bintan Island, Indonesia, over a 30-year period (1990–2020) using multi-temporal Landsat satellite imagery and the Random Forest classification algorithm. Landsat 5 and 8 imagery were pre-processed and classified to distinguish mangrove and non-mangrove areas, followed by accuracy assessment using ground-truth data collected in 2020. The classification achieved an overall accuracy of 97.06% and a Kappa coefficient of 0.96, confirming the robustness of the Random Forest approach for long-term coastal ecosystem monitoring. The results revealed a significant decline in mangrove cover in Bintan from 5,693 ha in 1990 to 4,817 ha in 2014, followed by an increase to 6,010 ha in 2018 and to 6,389 ha in 2020. Spatial analysis identified three main clusters of mangrove loss associated with anthropogenic pressures, those are (1) regional development (Dompak and Tanjung Uban), (2) bauxite mining (Teluk Bintan and Busung), and (3) private-sector domination (Air Raja and Tembeling). Meanwhile, areas such as Pengudang and Penaga showed mangrove recovery due to community-based rehabilitation programs and natural regeneration. The findings highlight that land conversion for infrastructure, mining, and industrial expansion remains the dominant driver of mangrove degradation in Bintan. However, successful restoration efforts demonstrate the potential for ecological recovery when effective management and rehabilitation strategies are implemented. This study emphasizes the value of combining remote sensing and machine learning techniques for long-term monitoring and sustainable management of coastal ecosystems in small island regions. Additionally, this finding provides critical spatial evidence to support coastal spatial planning, mangrove rehabililtation prioritization, and sustainable development policies in small island regions.
