AB  - In recent years, deep learning has demonstrated remarkable potential in hyperspectral image (HSI) classification. However, its training often relies on a large number of labeled samples. The annotation process for HSI is time-consuming and labor-intensive, making sample scarcity a major challenge for model training. To address this issue, we propose a Frequency Feature Siamese Network (F2SNet) for few-shot HSI classification. First, a frequency-enhanced fusion (FEF) module is designed to sequentially decompose the image into high, medium, and low-frequency components and enhance them. This approach enables the extraction of detail information across different frequency bands, amplifies feature distinctions, and strengthens critical information, resulting in more comprehensive and robust feature. Second, the Siamese network incorporates a combination of Transformer and convolutional neural networks (CNNs) for feature extraction, effectively capturing both local details and global contextual information. Additionally, an adaptive weighted contrastive loss is introduced to enhance intra-class compactness and inter-class separability in the feature space. Experimental results on the University of Pavia (PU) and Indian Pines (IP) datasets demonstrate that the proposed method achieves superior overall accuracy and generalization performance.
