AB  - The graph neural network (GNN) exhibits noteworthy performance in hyperspectral image classification (HSIC) due to its efficient message-passing structure, which employs the multilayer perceptron (MLP) for feature extraction and utilizes nonlinear activation function between two layers of the network. However, two significant limitations (poor model scaling and lack of interpretability) impede GNNs ability to extract deeper features in the graph. Therefore, this article proposes a novel method called graph Kolmogorov–Arnold convolutional network (GKACN) where the Kolmogorov–Arnold network (KAN) theory-based convolutional operation is employed in the graph for HSIC. As a new network architecture designed to replace the traditional MLP, KAN addresses the model scaling and interpretability limitations of MLP by using learnable spline-based functions in lieu of weights, resulting that GKACN fully exploits the potential of the KAN and GNN structures to facilitate the mining of more representative features. Moreover, textural features from the extended morphological attribute profile extraction are used for spatial–spectral classification. Comprehensive experiments on four distinct HSI datasets have demonstrated the feasibility and superiority of the proposed method compared with other state-of-the-art models.
