AB  - Lithological classification from hyperspectral images is essential for mineral exploration and geological mapping, but it faces challenges due to the high dimensionality of the data and the complexity of spectral signatures. This work introduces Adaptive PLSGeoSegNet, a novel hybrid deep neural network architecture for lithological segmentation. The main distinction of the proposed architecture is the incorporation of adaptive projection blocks inspired by the Partial Least Squares (PLS) method, which performs supervised spectral compression in an end-to-end manner during training. The effectiveness of Adaptive PLSGeoSegNet was validated on two distinct datasets: the public benchmark Pavia University and a new dataset from a geologically complex outcrop in the Neuquén Basin, Argentina. Compared to state-of-the-art architectures applied in the same context, the proposed model showed superior performance in Accuracy, F1-Score, and IoU metrics, achieving values of 88.77%, 86.63%, and 84.74%, respectively, on the generated dataset, thus offering a compact, interpretable, and efficient alternative for hyperspectral lithological mapping.
