AB  - Integrating remote sensing data with the Cellular Automata-Artificial Neural Network (CA-ANN) model provides an accurate and comprehensive method for assessing the extent of small-scale artisanal gold mining within forest reserves. Analysing and predicting the future extent of such mining activities can help develop effective monitoring and management practices. This chapter aims to utilise remote sensing data and the CA-ANN model to forecast the level of mining activities occurring in forest reserves in Ghana. This would provide valuable insights to support sustainable forest management practices. The objectives are: (i) to map and analyse the extent of mining in the Oda River forest reserve for the epochs of 2013, 2016, 2020, and 2023 and (ii) to predict the future extent of mining in the reserve for 2033 using a CA-ANN model. To achieve the first objective, Landsat-8 images for the selected years were preprocessed and classified into seven land cover classes. The second objective involved identifying the physical proximate factors, which include the digital elevation model, slope, and distance to rivers, roads, and communities, for the modelling and simulation of transition potential. Using the five proximate variables, the CA-ANN approach and the MLP technique were employed to predict the 2033 land cover map based on the 2013 and 2023 maps. Finally, a post-classification change detection technique was used to analyse changes in land cover. The results showed that mining activity in the region increased by 6.17% over a decade, leading to a yearly deforestation rate of 107.487 ha and a total loss of 944.37 ha of forest. Furthermore, the study revealed that illegal miners exploited weaknesses in reserve monitoring systems during the COVID-19 pandemic lockdown, enabling them to operate without restrictions and substantially expand their activities. This led to an approximately 20% increase in mining in the reserve compared to the previous period. The study predicts that if the current trend persists, mining activity in the Oda River Forest Reserve will expand by 244.08 ha by 2033, resulting in devastating impacts on the forest cover. Over the next 10 years, a net loss of 930.6 ha of forest is projected, with 201.42 ha attributed specifically to mining activities. However, the study also indicates that the projected increase in mining activities within the next decade will be lower than the increase observed during the COVID-19 lockdown period. The study highlights the critical role of surveillance, legal enforcement, and regulatory frameworks in protecting forest reserves from mining activities. In conclusion, the CA-ANN technology holds significant potential for enhancing the management and conservation of Ghana’s forest reserves while mitigating the adverse environmental impacts of small-scale gold mining.
