AB  - Highlights: What are the main findings? A Random Forest classifier trained on K-means pseudo-labels reproduces spectral clustering with >99.9% fidelity, enabling automated mapping without manual annotation. Automated Snowline Altitude (SLA) extraction using a high-confidence threshold ((Formula presented.)) achieves strong agreement with field-derived ELA ((Formula presented.), RMSE (Formula presented.) m). What are the implications of the main findings? The hybrid workflow provides a validated, operational proxy for glacier mass balance in data-sparse Arctic regions where in situ records are absent. High-threshold elevation binning mitigates mixed-pixel errors in 10 m satellite imagery, offering a validated alternative to standard thresholding methods. Accurate equilibrium-line altitude (ELA) estimates are a valuable proxy for evaluating glacier mass balance conditions and interpreting climate-driven change in the Canadian high Arctic, where sustained in situ observations are limited. A scalable remote-sensing framework is evaluated to extract the snow cover ratio (SCR) and snowline altitude (SLA) on White Glacier (Axel Heiberg Island, Nunavut) and to assess the agreement with in situ ELA measurements. Ten-metre Sentinel-2 imagery (2019–2024) is processed with a hybrid pipeline comprising the principal component analysis (PCA) of four bands (B2, B3, B4, and B8), unsupervised K-means for pseudo-label generation, and a Random Forest (RF) classifier for snow/ice/ground mapping. SLA is defined based on the date of seasonal minimum SCR using (i) a snowline pixel elevation histogram (SPEH; mode) and (ii) elevation binning with SCR thresholds (0.5 and 0.8). Validation against field-derived ELAs (2019–2023) is performed; formal SLA precision from DEM and binning is quantified ((Formula presented.) m), and associations with positive degree days (PDDs) at Eureka are examined. The RF classifier reproduces the spectral clustering structure with >99.9% fidelity. Elevation binning at (Formula presented.) yields SLAs closely matching field ELAs (Pearson (Formula presented.), (Formula presented.) ; RMSE (Formula presented.) m), whereas SPEH and lower-threshold binning are less accurate. Interannual variability is pronounced as follows: minimum SCR spans 0.46–0.76 and co-varies with SLA; correlations with PDDs are positive but modest. Results indicate that high-threshold elevation-bin filtering with machine learning provides a reliable proxy for ELA in clean-ice settings, with potential transferability to other data-sparse Arctic sites, while underscoring the importance of image timing and mixed-pixel effects in residual SLA–ELA differences.
