AB  - A novel method called Graph Kolmogorov-Arnold Convolutional Network (GKACN) is proposed in this study, in which the convolutional operation based on Kolmogorov-Arnold Network (KAN) theory is employed in the graph for hyperspectral image classification (HSIC). The Graph Neural Network (GNN) exhibits noteworthy performance in HSIC due to its efficient message passing structure, which employs the Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) for feature extraction and utilizes nonlinear activation function between two layers of the network. As a new neural network architecture designed to replace traditional MLP, KAN addresses the model scaling and interpretability limitations of MLP by employing learnable spline-based functions in lieu of weights. The GKACN fully exploits the potential of the KAN and GNN structures to facilitate the mining of more representative features. Moreover, textural features based on extended morphological attribute profile (EMAP) extraction are further been applied to the model to realize the union of spatial-spectral information and enhance the accuracy of classification. The effectiveness of the proposed approach has been assessed through experiments conducted on two high-quality datasets with limited samples.
