AB  - Accurate extraction of water bodies from multispectral remote sensing imagery remains challenging in urban regions with complex hydrological structures and heterogeneous land cover. To address this issue, this study proposes a feature fusion approach that integrates AlphaEarth Foundation (AEF) embeddings with multispectral data. Given the small size of the labeled dataset in this study, we compared three data sources (Sentinel-2, EnMAP, and AEF) across three models (1D-CNN, LSTM, and MLP) for classification performance. Experimental results show that AEF achieved an overall accuracy 0.6 percentage points higher than EnMAP and 1.3 percentage points higher than Sentinel-2. From the model perspective, differences among the three models were minor, with MLP slightly outperforming 1D-CNN and LSTM. However, in inversion tasks, MLP demonstrated superior performance compared to the other two models. Consequently, MLP was used for feature extraction in the fusion process, followed by weighted integration. Notably, while the overall accuracy of the EnMAP+AEF fusion on the sample dataset was 1 percentage point higher than that of the Sentinel-2 +AEF fusion, the inversion results of Sentinel-2 +AEF were better. These results indicate that higher classification accuracy does not necessarily translate into better inversion performance. The experiments conducted in this study provide provides a useful reference for water body extraction in urban regions with complex river network systems using multi-source remote sensing data.
