AB  - Landslide phenomena constitute a major geohazard across China's orographic landscapes, imposing substantial risks on population security and natural resources, notably in areas of concentrated mineral extraction. To address the inefficiencies inherent in traditional manual identification and computer vision based methods, and considering the balance between accuracy and speed across the entire YOLO series for landslide detection, we selected YOLOv8 as the baseline model. Leveraging the efficient YOLOv8 architecture, we introduce DKF-YOLO, a novel algorithm designed for accurate and efficient landslide identification in remote sensing imagery. The development commenced with establishing the Liulin landslide dataset, curated through a two-year effort by collecting tens of thousands of raw images and rigorously annotating the final samples. We then designed a Multi-level Hierarchical Kernel Aggregation Network (MHKA-Net) module to dynamically adjust the receptive field, enabling efficient extraction of multi-scale contextual information for precise landslide localization. In addition, we developed an Attention-based Internal Scale Feature Interaction (AISFI) module to replace the original SPPF module in the YOLOv8 backbone. This module enhanced feature interaction at higher levels, thereby improving the extraction of fine-grained features for landslides of different shapes and sizes. On the Liulin landslide dataset, our model achieved 94.4% precision and 95.5% recall at the instance level—outperforming 8 state-of-the art benchmarks. Notably, it delivered improvements of +3.0% precision and +5.4% recall relative to the second-best model. The model also demonstrated robust generalizability on publicly available Bijie Landslide dataset and an efficient solution for landslide monitoring.
