AB  - The rapid desiccation of Lake Urmia, a critical regional water body, presents a stark example of the environmental consequences of climate change and unsustainable water management. This study aims to investigate the impact of Lake Urmia’s desiccation on regional cooling effects from 1986 to 2035. Using the remote sensing images of Landsat 5, 7 and 8 satellites and combined method of object-oriented-pixel based land use classification in 7 time periods (1986, 1992, 1998, 2004, 2010, 2016 and 2023) and prediction of future land use changes (2029 and 2035) was conducted with ANN-CA algorithm. The average temperature of the Earth’s surface was calculated in the buffers of 5 km and 30 m around Lake Urmia. Also, by using Random Forest, Extra trees, Decision tree, Ridge, Lasso, Linear Regression, SVM, KNN, and XGB regression machine learning algorithms, the relationship between the surface temperature of the Earth and environmental and human variables was investigated, and the surface temperature of the earth in 2029 and 2035 was also predicted. The results showed that the area of Lake Urmia was 515,174.04 ha in 1986, which decreased to 64,620.36 ha in 2023, and is expected to reach 51,309.27 ha in 2035. The classification of the land surface temperature for the years 2029 and 2035 showed that the land surface temperature class above 50 degrees Celsius, which covers 43.03 % of the region in 2023, will increase to 69.22 % of the entire region in 2035. The distance of the cooling effect showed that the maximum cooling of the lake was in 1998 (1050 m); this distance decreased to 90 (2023) meters, which indicates a drastic change in the cooling effect of the lake.
