AB  - Hyperspectral image (HSI) has rich spectral features that can be used in several applications. However, due to the environment and imaging mechanisms, HSI is prone to noise. Several CNN (convolutional neural network) based methods explore the spectral correlation of the bands for denoising. Unfortunately, it fails to obtain high and low-level features across different bands. In addition, traditional CNN lacks global attention to the spectral and spatial features, which makes the reconstructed image unclear and gives less information on the land cover classes. In this study, we combine the strengths of the 3DCNN and 3D-ViT in HSTNet (hybrid spectral transformer network) for HSI denoising. The 3DCNN is capable of extracting high-dimensional spectral features; at the same time, it increases the computation costs. Therefore, we design LSSFM (local spectral and spatial feature module) in which the spatial domain utilizes two-dimensional filters to extract features independently for each spectral band. In the 3D-ViT, we incorporated LSSA (local spectral self-attention) to provide attention and global attention to the spectral and spatial features. The LSSA is a three-dimensional self-attention (3D-SA) in the spectral dimension, distinct from the conventional SA (self-attention), which emphasizes spatial or channel dimensions. The focus on spectral dimensions enables LSSA to demonstrate a linear computing complexity despite being able to represent long-range connections within HSI data. Furthermore, we evaluated the model performance on the real IP, PU and synthetic dataset ICVL. The HSTNet achieved SAM (spectral angle mapper) values of 1.68, 1.98, 1.99 and 2.32 for Non-i.i.d. Gaussian, Gaussian + Deadline, Gaussian + Stripe, and Gaussian + Impulse noise on the ICVL dataset. In addition, our model obtained a kappa value of 97.12 % and 88.65 %, after noise removal on the Pavia University and Indiana Pines datasets.
